1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,460
It is 960 AD

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:05,190
You haven't slept in three days.

3
00:00:05,290 --> 00:00:14,840
Your head is pounding, your breasts are engorged, and your hands are shaking so violently you're afraid you'll drop the porcelain bowl in front of you.

4
00:00:15,340 --> 00:00:16,390
Why?

5
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:23,300
Because the Emperor's third concubine is convinced the Empress is trying to murder her newborn son.

6
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:30,130
And in the Inner Court of the Song Dynasty, a jealous wife doesn't target the baby directly.

7
00:00:30,230 --> 00:00:31,990
She targets the milk.

8
00:00:32,490 --> 00:00:37,430
Which means before a single drop touches the lips of the infant prince...

9
00:00:37,530 --> 00:00:39,940
you have to taste-test it first.

10
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:43,570
You take a breath, staring down at the white liquid.

11
00:00:43,670 --> 00:00:47,200
If the Empress succeeded, this is your last meal.

12
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:57,570
How did you find yourself in the most dangerous room in the empire?

13
00:00:57,670 --> 00:01:02,270
Just three months ago, you were a peasant woman in a muddy village.

14
00:01:02,370 --> 00:01:05,300
You had just given birth to your own child.

15
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:09,690
But in the Imperial Court, a woman's milk is a commodity.

16
00:01:10,190 --> 00:01:23,810
When the Emperor's favorite concubine fell pregnant, her eunuchs scoured the provinces looking for women with the perfect "constitution." They inspected your skin, your teeth, and the health of your own baby.

17
00:01:23,910 --> 00:01:25,410
You were chosen.

18
00:01:25,910 --> 00:01:27,630
But there was a price.

19
00:01:27,730 --> 00:01:32,370
To feed a prince, you had to leave your own child behind in the mud.

20
00:01:32,470 --> 00:01:41,010
You were brought through the massive vermilion gates of the palace, stripped, scrubbed, and dressed in silks you aren't even allowed to keep.

21
00:01:41,510 --> 00:01:45,480
You quickly learned the brutal hierarchy of the Harem.

22
00:01:45,580 --> 00:01:50,970
The Lady who hired you is a consort, a glorified second-class wife.

23
00:01:51,070 --> 00:01:58,310
Her entire existence, her safety, and her wealth depend on the survival of the boy she just birthed.

24
00:01:58,410 --> 00:02:02,530
If he lives, she might become an Empress Dowager one day.

25
00:02:02,630 --> 00:02:04,970
If he dies, she is nothing.

26
00:02:05,070 --> 00:02:06,290
And you?

27
00:02:06,390 --> 00:02:10,150
You are just the vessel keeping her ambition alive.

28
00:02:11,267 --> 00:02:18,787
You thought living in a palace meant luxury, but you quickly realize you are a prisoner of your own biology.

29
00:02:18,887 --> 00:02:22,047
The court physicians watch you like a hawk.

30
00:02:22,547 --> 00:02:27,827
You are legally forbidden from eating garlic, onions, or heavy spices.

31
00:02:27,927 --> 00:02:33,207
They believe "hot" foods will sour your milk and corrupt the royal bloodline.

32
00:02:33,307 --> 00:02:40,437
Every day, you are forced to eat bland, unseasoned pork fat and fish soup to keep your milk rich.

33
00:02:40,937 --> 00:02:44,227
You are forbidden from showing anger or grief.

34
00:02:44,327 --> 00:02:51,017
The doctors believe if you cry, your milk will curdle with poison and give the young master colic.

35
00:02:51,117 --> 00:02:54,517
If the baby gets a fever, it's not bad luck.

36
00:02:54,617 --> 00:02:56,077
It's your fault.

37
00:02:56,177 --> 00:02:57,847
You will be beaten.

38
00:02:58,967 --> 00:03:04,437
But the physical restrictions are nothing compared to the psychological warfare.

39
00:03:04,537 --> 00:03:07,847
Look out the lattice window of the nursery.

40
00:03:07,947 --> 00:03:11,177
See those women walking in the courtyard?

41
00:03:11,277 --> 00:03:14,507
That is the Empress and her entourage.

42
00:03:14,607 --> 00:03:16,257
She has no sons.

43
00:03:16,357 --> 00:03:21,257
Her womb is empty, and her biological clock is ticking against her.

44
00:03:21,357 --> 00:03:26,437
Every time she looks toward your nursery, your stomach twists into knots.

45
00:03:26,937 --> 00:03:29,487
Back to the bowl in your hands.

46
00:03:29,587 --> 00:03:35,527
The concubine is hovering over you, her eyes wild with postpartum paranoia.

47
00:03:35,627 --> 00:03:42,497
She swears she saw a kitchen eunuch whispering to one of the Empress's maids near the well this morning.

48
00:03:42,997 --> 00:03:45,057
"Drink," she whispers to you.

49
00:03:45,157 --> 00:03:50,627
Her voice is trembling, but her hand is on the hilt of a guard's dagger.

50
00:03:51,127 --> 00:03:54,207
You bring the cool porcelain to your lips.

51
00:03:54,307 --> 00:04:00,847
You know that if the Empress did bribe the kitchen staff, they likely used arsenic or crushed lead.

52
00:04:00,947 --> 00:04:03,467
It won't taste like anything.

53
00:04:03,567 --> 00:04:04,897
You swallow.

54
00:04:05,397 --> 00:04:07,307
One second passes.

55
00:04:07,407 --> 00:04:08,417
Two.

56
00:04:08,517 --> 00:04:11,187
Your heart thumps against your ribs.

57
00:04:11,287 --> 00:04:14,137
You wait for the burning in your throat.

58
00:04:14,237 --> 00:04:16,827
You wait for your stomach to cramp.

59
00:04:17,327 --> 00:04:18,977
Nothing happens.

60
00:04:19,077 --> 00:04:22,757
Your lungs expand as you take a shaky breath.

61
00:04:22,857 --> 00:04:24,057
It's safe.

62
00:04:24,157 --> 00:04:25,527
For today.

63
00:04:26,027 --> 00:04:35,107
The concubine snatches the bowl from your hands, her face instantly shifting from terror to cold, aristocratic indifference.

64
00:04:35,207 --> 00:04:40,827
She cradles her son and feeds him, completely forgetting you are even in the room.

65
00:04:40,927 --> 00:04:45,607
You step back into the shadows of the silk curtains, wiping your mouth.

66
00:04:45,707 --> 00:04:47,927
You survived the morning.

67
00:04:48,027 --> 00:04:51,387
But the prince has to eat again in four hours.

68
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:57,420
This was the hidden reality of the women who built dynasties.

69
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:03,410
History books remember the Emperors, the grand battles, and the beautiful Empresses.

70
00:05:03,510 --> 00:05:09,640
But the foundations of these empires were built on the backs of forgotten peasant women.

71
00:05:10,140 --> 00:05:20,070
Women like you, who traded their own children's lives for a chance to survive in a golden cage, risking death with every single feeding.

72
00:05:20,570 --> 00:05:27,680
When we look at the glitz and glamour of ancient empires, we have to ask ourselves: who paid the price for it?
